#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e761b5-ec1c-549d-b726-e0909cfe460b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line in Parametric Form and a Plane in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1bd5231-6855-55f3-a5ec-61c58423a2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399630} {:answer/id #uuid "3a87675b-c089-5d14-9db0-504805f8a6af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399631} {:answer/id #uuid "02bc6063-6eef-56d8-b50b-eec9ca9b7e38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399632} {:answer/id #uuid "b949434a-7780-5a3d-b4d8-3bbde783f79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399633} {:answer/id #uuid "44390ca7-20ca-5d21-b624-15837dd00ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bd5231-6855-55f3-a5ec-61c58423a2bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,1,9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399630} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8515a18-0ebe-5740-9abf-0e5d9167649f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60f0cd70-b4b4-5b35-85ba-563cceee9aad" :question/math-academy-id "q-108326" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2t \\\\ y=5+t \\\\ z=-3-3t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:5x+3y-4z=-43.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3y-4z & =-43 \\\\ 5(6+2t)+3(5+t)-4(-3-3t) & =-43 \\\\ 30+10t+15+3t+12+12t & =-43 \\\\ 25t & =-100 \\\\ t & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=-4$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6+2(-4) \\\\ y=5+(-4) \\\\ z=-3-3(-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(-2,1,9).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82e761b5-ec1c-549d-b726-e0909cfe460b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line in Parametric Form and a Plane in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cbd933f-3f29-5070-8231-31ef77e6f4c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(20,29,40)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399637} {:answer/id #uuid "f073d53a-a8e7-5ae2-82c8-e5cb31403d9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-23,-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399638} {:answer/id #uuid "654434c9-d7c4-5e1e-bfe8-aaafc9425c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-7,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399639} {:answer/id #uuid "509a688f-9e49-51a5-95ba-17bf03e47002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399640} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ec3fa5-fe3a-5ef1-bb6e-18d2712b8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,9,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1ec3fa5-fe3a-5ef1-bb6e-18d2712b8636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10,9,15)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399641} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4bd1c19-a96b-51b8-b83f-2c4bebba5b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3ee9813a-b1b3-5ef0-86f7-ac30b2000775" :question/math-academy-id "q-108368" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2t \\\\ y=-3+4t \\\\ z=5t\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,Π:3x+2y-z=33.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We can substitute the parametric expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ of the line into the equation of the plane immediately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y-z & =33 \\\\ 3(4+2t)+2(-3+4t)-(5t) & =33 \\\\ 12+6t-6+8t-5t & =33 \\\\ 9t & =27 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P$, we substitute $t=3$ into the parametric equations of the line:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4+2(3)=10 \\\\ y=-3+4(3)=9 \\\\ z=5(3)=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $P(10,9,15).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd2ac48-7620-52cc-95be-704f08884c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,-3,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399644} {:answer/id #uuid "bdec2734-53b3-5679-b71d-75f8bcbc08d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399645} {:answer/id #uuid "546f5e56-43c2-5956-b6c9-0175baee1711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399646} {:answer/id #uuid "65add1a9-10b0-59ed-9c8d-f16bb33c3988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399647} {:answer/id #uuid "25644b17-ee2a-5eb9-ae20-64906545cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14,-4,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25644b17-ee2a-5eb9-ae20-64906545cc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(14,-4,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399648} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e560f3d-0487-5833-a63c-2ddd954cbbe7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0da8771f-0983-5e77-be97-afe167de38df" :question/math-academy-id "q-108371" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩,\\,Π:r=⟨10,-5,12⟩+s⟨4,3,0⟩+t⟨0,2,5⟩.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of a plane in the parametric form $r=p+tv+sw$, we have that its normal vector is $n=v\\times w.$ In our case, the normal vector to $Π$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =⟨4,3,0⟩\\times ⟨0,2,5⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =15i-20j+8k \\\\  & =⟨15,-20,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane $Π$ in dot product form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =⟨10,-5,12⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨4,-2,3⟩+w⟨5,-1,2⟩$ into the equation of the plane in dot product form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ ⟨4+5w,-2-w,3+2w⟩\\cdot ⟨15,-20,8⟩ & =346 \\\\ 15(4+5w)-20(-2-w)+8(3+2w) & =346 \\\\ 60+75w+40+20w+24+16w & =346 \\\\ 111w & =222 \\\\ w & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $q$ of the intersection point $Q,$ we substitute $w=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨4+5(2),-2-(2),3+2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨14,-4,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection point is $Q(14,-4,7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f4e3599-287a-56a3-8f1b-9e7033803e0a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Acceleration on a Speed-Time Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f114a5-8479-5ab2-898f-e1a0d9b64d84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $20\\,\\text{s}$ to $40\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399651} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfd7b7f-f09a-57f0-9cdf-fa0624b62f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399652} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf8f966-bf45-522b-9a34-c98c57a6aa01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399653} {:answer/id #uuid "2463de37-8db5-51e1-909c-4ac15ec53855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $10\\,\\text{s}$ to $50\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399654} {:answer/id #uuid "8f078bce-f94d-54cc-ae41-aea8efc48b13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $0\\,\\text{s}$ to $30\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bfd7b7f-f09a-57f0-9cdf-fa0624b62f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "From $30\\,\\text{s}$ to $60\\,\\text{s}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399652} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d62422f-d84e-5e31-bc10-667678bf2be5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c47c1778-9762-5608-84e1-7206031f9c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-108462" :question/problem "![](images/31/31e0e843eb581f01cdc7ebb5273398a3c0f32d2949b9e36cd28423295d7b76e8.png)\n\nThe speed-time graph shown above represents the motion of a $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle. At what times is the motorcycle accelerating?" :question/worked-solution "The motorcycle is accelerating when its acceleration is positive. In a speed-time graph, the acceleration is given by the slope of the line. So, we need to find the times for which the slope is positive.\n\nWe see from the graph that the slope is positive from $t=30\\,\\text{s}$ to $t=60\\,\\text{s}.$ Therefore, the $250\\text{cc}$ motorcycle is accelerating in this time interval."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf29808-4551-56a0-ac86-a4587a06fc0f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection Point of a Line and a Plane Given in Other Forms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c181d54f-7b5e-5e40-b660-5db606239a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(13,7,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399658} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2c2f01-60e6-5197-ae47-76dd272575a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399659} {:answer/id #uuid "100fc206-faa0-5c9a-8c1d-f828c27459ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,-5,-13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} {:answer/id #uuid "a03ac96b-8e87-5579-959e-6059ecf3486c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,-3,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399661} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d8eeca-91ef-5d44-a9a1-da7d1707ffce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No intersection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "100fc206-faa0-5c9a-8c1d-f828c27459ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,-5,-13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399660} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd4a0b3-55ce-54d3-98c8-8b7021d46e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d225520-d7e9-5a28-afbf-6e16563a3611" :question/math-academy-id "q-108889" :question/problem "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z+4}{3},\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩=39.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(3,1,-4)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=⟨5,2,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨3,1,-4⟩+t⟨5,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩ & =39 \\\\ ⟨3+5t,1+2t,-4+3t⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,2,-1⟩ & =39 \\\\ (-3)(3+5t)+2(1+2t)-(-4+3t) & =39 \\\\ -9-15t+2+4t+4-3t & =39 \\\\ -14t & =42 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨3+5(-3),1+2(-3),-4+3(-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-12,-5,-13⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(-12,-5,-13).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6773b545-4c85-529f-8c68-3b883278b649" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{C-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399665} {:answer/id #uuid "f60a96fe-942a-5e12-a66c-9e48bde3cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399666} {:answer/id #uuid "fe66776a-5049-554f-b295-b90565404145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{C+2{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399667} {:answer/id #uuid "e00a7e53-ea54-5b25-9e84-5594638cadcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{3}+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399668} {:answer/id #uuid "f849933c-eec0-5349-a930-557222d316e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60a96fe-942a-5e12-a66c-9e48bde3cb0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399666} :answer-field/id #uuid "782ea541-65bb-5fe7-a7fe-a730b04302d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8cbfb9b8-f301-5858-97fc-1b90fa72d9ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-109158" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xy \\\\ {x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & ={x}^{3}{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{{y}^{-1}}{x}=x.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, equation $(∗)$ can be written as a linear equation in $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}z=x\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-x\n$$\n\nThe above equation is now in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{3x}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5103ad76-a94a-5286-8ba5-a77f53f60465" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399672} {:answer/id #uuid "8436223b-700a-5cfc-999b-c15dd1c4967a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399673} {:answer/id #uuid "48bbfde1-4a90-5800-90a2-a13b81b7ffae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399674} {:answer/id #uuid "dbee7bb0-5392-5018-a5d9-d29c6d9994e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399675} {:answer/id #uuid "9445423e-a521-575c-b99b-639347c58fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8436223b-700a-5cfc-999b-c15dd1c4967a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399673} :answer-field/id #uuid "689f3bd0-df18-5a92-92e6-a4a5162ad010" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6414dac4-9443-5e38-b246-ae2ca315e478" :question/math-academy-id "q-109302" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+3t-2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-18t+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6{t}^{2}-18t+3) \\\\  & =12t-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{18}{12} \\\\ t & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{3}{2}) & =6{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-18(\\frac{3}{2})+3 \\\\  & =6(\\frac{9}{4})-27+3 \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-27+3 \\\\  & =-\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bee6d4bf-79da-5eb3-a214-8c045dbce5bd" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Velocity of a Particle at the Moment When Its Acceleration is Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1f8e3ed-64ac-534b-9097-97dd33f2545d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{3}{2},\\,v=-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399679} {:answer/id #uuid "106f9321-de03-58f4-8540-f31885a755c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399680} {:answer/id #uuid "084bda93-d0d6-514b-b8c7-73ce821b2053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399681} {:answer/id #uuid "367c159c-f8cf-54ab-9cdc-cfddc4ed2a5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399682} {:answer/id #uuid "212ea060-615f-5258-8e09-ac350ea1ca59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{15}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "084bda93-d0d6-514b-b8c7-73ce821b2053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t=\\frac{7}{3},\\,v=\\frac{49}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399681} :answer-field/id #uuid "1669c4fe-448a-5ecd-96c2-60941d083824" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7704a427-55d1-5f8e-bf57-d941d8544127" :question/math-academy-id "q-109307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment." :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7{t}^{2}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =14t-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(14t-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =14-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14-6t & =0 \\\\ -6t & =-14 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the acceleration is zero when $t=\\frac{7}{3}.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{7}{3}) & =14(\\frac{7}{3})-3{(\\frac{7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{98}{3}-\\frac{49}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c19ef7ec-19a3-533a-bcd9-1b775f301801" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a Homogeneous Initial Value Problem to a First-Order System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af7079ea-861b-500c-9d7f-1e6deedf2499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15{x}_{1}+12{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399686} {:answer/id #uuid "6b240488-157e-59e5-bed1-15811d8bfd83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{e}^{t}{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399687} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2b02a7-5e76-599c-b59b-0e2513d27664" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{x}_{1}-15{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399688} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a5f6c5-4aef-5b2b-b492-7a62db70e223" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{e}^{t}{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399689} {:answer/id #uuid "9224e1b1-aac6-5da0-9fca-0ec4d20218c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{x}_{1}-4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af7079ea-861b-500c-9d7f-1e6deedf2499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15{x}_{1}+12{e}^{t}{x}_{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399686} :answer-field/id #uuid "8605a456-8f74-549b-ba68-da660540b8c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebf9231a-650d-5b8c-8818-efc1d0c42664" :question/math-academy-id "q-109639" :question/problem "The second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}+5y=0,\\,y(0)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1\n$$\n\ncan be written as a system of first-order differential equations, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=p \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=f(t),\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}(t)=y(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t)={y}^{′}(t),$ and $p$ and $q$ are constants. Calculate  $(p-q)\\cdot f(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =y \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={y}^{′}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also convert the initial conditions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & ={x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(0) & ={x}_{2}(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) & ={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt this point, we're almost done. We just need to write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{y}^{′}-5y.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{1}(0)=2 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2},\\, & {x}_{2}(0)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $p=2,q=-1,f(t)=-5{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2},$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(p-q)\\cdot f(t) & =(2-(-1))(-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =3(-5{x}_{1}+4{e}^{t}{x}_{2}) \\\\  & =-15{x}_{1}+12{e}^{t}{x}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "42525832-e186-538f-baf8-bb4418e9820b" :knowledge-point/title "Working With Systems Containing Inequalities Presented in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84a02541-33c6-5f69-a920-35696f66c170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\le -8 \\\\ y\\ge \\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399693} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb29637-b617-5711-9aa5-c2814f5eceb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y>-8 \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399694} {:answer/id #uuid "0642a65f-b668-5a8d-b6f2-b2575f8c1362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\ge \\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399695} {:answer/id #uuid "f8ff8a6d-ad42-5c71-989f-6bb5f699155a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\le -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399696} {:answer/id #uuid "f8efb9aa-532e-55ae-9077-3bedc400e006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8efb9aa-532e-55ae-9077-3bedc400e006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399697} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce68956-d8ec-5373-80c1-0576c6b35476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9a72356-7e05-5f10-b6ac-a2418c3b4fa6" :question/math-academy-id "q-110078" :question/problem "![](images/39/3905dacc3977857933a48ced329fc245b357f732aec520e67998efc0b5d7cdab.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" :question/worked-solution "Consider the line $x+2y=-8.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Writing the equation of this line in slope-intercept form, we have\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2}x-4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -\\frac{1}{2}x-4,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nx+2y\\ge -8.\n$$\n\nNow consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y\\ge -8 \\\\ y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0303bd06-294e-5f7a-bbd2-b12abdeb93c5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 3x3 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ca0c6a8-b2d4-544c-89bf-7a6bdb87675a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399700} {:answer/id #uuid "f5dff314-51c8-5797-918b-20e449b957ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399701} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c19027-5aab-5875-9dab-62a9301471b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399702} {:answer/id #uuid "aa718d78-9395-5d1b-bac6-f86073e47af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399703} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7785c4-6c2c-5f23-92b4-7555437dc106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa718d78-9395-5d1b-bac6-f86073e47af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399703} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a779cd-5bb0-57e3-8fd6-1d037d1225d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34a82bff-05f0-5ea6-9b78-0f59cb6e7dc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-110294" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an elementary matrix is obtained by performing exactly one elementary row operation on the identity matrix $I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from $I$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from $I$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $B$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44ff35b-4cca-5963-8d55-0e4af2771950" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be97cf6c-4556-5536-81b7-f6cbe24c54a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399707} {:answer/id #uuid "117335d8-eef6-57cc-80c4-717d7d851e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399708} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6b5e0b-d411-52b9-a442-c37893725b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399709} {:answer/id #uuid "39da6146-c209-5992-b91d-37d9cbcc968d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399710} {:answer/id #uuid "b297110d-c4a4-5370-8af2-eb3719806737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be97cf6c-4556-5536-81b7-f6cbe24c54a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399707} :answer-field/id #uuid "96dde3e3-d566-5414-b2ac-0401b768ea42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1e236ec-23c7-5eb7-a830-7a2578db1027" :question/math-academy-id "q-110554" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-2,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(1) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only \n$z$\nis an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+2I)z=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+2I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f44ff35b-4cca-5963-8d55-0e4af2771950" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baaf111f-ecaf-5dfb-9f65-942bc71c6554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399714} {:answer/id #uuid "b3712664-f7b3-543b-b032-53f4f7262d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399715} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4d0f2c-995b-56c0-92a5-99a9f17181ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399716} {:answer/id #uuid "fb7ee83c-de92-5377-b9ac-5a738cb237e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399717} {:answer/id #uuid "6d14f2c9-cfe8-585a-be92-5036ed36cef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3712664-f7b3-543b-b032-53f4f7262d5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399715} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a59f474-95b3-51b1-a3ee-0b7d7cc3bba4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcd0c175-5061-5e65-ae35-dc31f2b4c302" :question/math-academy-id "q-110569" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(-1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- one $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-1,$ and\n\n- one $1\\times 1$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(2) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-1.$ \n\nNow, computing $A+I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A+I)y=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=z,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A+I)^{2}y=(A+I)z=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}-12 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A+I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "269a38f4-d50d-507d-a2fe-b6b9552ccaae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399721} {:answer/id #uuid "e06b713f-a822-5e21-a364-ba83171f6e15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399722} {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fb2b4-2519-5080-98a6-8275a55a992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399723} {:answer/id #uuid "c53343ca-8e57-512e-ba07-1f08dda987cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399724} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab7ea6-d839-514b-b71d-a9aadeb68fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a1fb2b4-2519-5080-98a6-8275a55a992e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399723} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c3251d1-6964-5600-84fe-fbb97c423ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b32c3e49-95fd-5233-9aa7-ff2e20c39b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-110754" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399726 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f726da6-7b87-558e-8b51-b7840bdc0abe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399728} {:answer/id #uuid "dfad63f4-b83c-5439-a2d9-6fc118ca836e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399729} {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bb0c-f3dd-57f2-a3a9-f151350734f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399730} {:answer/id #uuid "421e6c1d-b7d5-5d36-a5a5-f3ccd5c75076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399731} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfb14db-bf18-5acc-8ed0-cb564150d8a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b8bb0c-f3dd-57f2-a3a9-f151350734f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399730} :answer-field/id #uuid "af12360f-3ae7-5782-b9f9-7e9d6d3e8888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4400c4d4-e4b9-52ce-9f78-148918755004" :question/math-academy-id "q-110756" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ and ${a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f53fa522-6d50-51d9-abdd-62735575a2d1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Vector of a Stochastic 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399734} {:answer/id #uuid "1f57ef16-daf6-55cf-b3cb-f0b639c205a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399735} {:answer/id #uuid "d2cdee49-7ce2-5cce-acee-cd378fd6b19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399736} {:answer/id #uuid "4c833b2d-9013-504c-b338-e5d0dc7d7a90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399737} {:answer/id #uuid "11a1059c-7f59-5f04-b1af-4e76b82a2739" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33a2da09-ade9-5f02-bc30-6ec862b4c502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399734} :answer-field/id #uuid "6866c1e5-6616-5f78-9eb2-94b16182edd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c92fa7-df07-5594-a6b7-bd705bfe80a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-111172" :question/problem "The migration table below describes the movement of the population between cities and suburbs every year in a certain state. \n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Cities** | **Suburbs** |\n| To: | **Cities** | $0.8$ | $0.7$ |\n| **Suburbs** | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |  |\n\nThat is, $20%$ of those living in cities move to the suburbs each year, and $70%$ of those living in suburbs move to the cities.\n\nAssuming that the population of the state is $9$ million and remains constant, how many people would eventually live in cities if the given probabilities remain constant over many years?" :question/worked-solution "From the table, we get the corresponding stochastic matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ contains only positive entries. So, the matrix $P$ is regular. As a result, after many years, the distribution will eventually converge to the unique steady-state vector of $P,$ and this convergence does not depend on the initial state.\n\nRecall that a steady-state vector of a stochastic matrix $P$ is a stochastic vector $q$ such that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Pq=q\\,⟹\\,(P-I)q=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $P-I,$ we get\n\n$$\nP-I=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(P-I)q=0$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0.2 & -0.7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-0.2 & 0.7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the steady-state vector must be stochastic, its entries should add up to $1.$ Therefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nq=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{9} \\\\ \\frac{2}{9}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, after many years, $\\frac{7}{9}$ of the $9$ million population will live in cities. Therefore, the total number of people living in cities after many years will be\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot 9\\,\\text{million}=7\\,\\text{million}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6197fd27-ebb4-589c-a332-928ce1beea75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399740} {:answer/id #uuid "c382c06e-6be3-50d3-afd6-cfb547faf5c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399741} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab49bdc-91a7-5f81-ae26-3dd0344e8f20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399742} {:answer/id #uuid "c6333bf7-6d20-5d03-afb9-8cf92d484a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399743} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6faf66-9395-59a4-b653-5f44c8a58eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6333bf7-6d20-5d03-afb9-8cf92d484a71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399743} :answer-field/id #uuid "af77e6f6-d746-55ef-8323-11da194a2c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-112432" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(0).$ \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(0) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is an eigenvector of $A,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-0I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-0I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88f1c5a8-42ee-5523-84ec-f29f28e0ac2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-264\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=-14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399747} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2399df-9166-5676-8f9c-a51959d4ab11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=343\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=-14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399748} {:answer/id #uuid "dff389cf-80e0-5f10-b6f2-86dcbc11e52c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-14\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=49$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399749} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d9b4a-b38e-5fab-ad4f-cb09b4aea808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-343\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=-14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399750} {:answer/id #uuid "7dec2dec-aebb-5a50-befe-935b17a9002a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-343\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dec2dec-aebb-5a50-befe-935b17a9002a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-343\\,$ and $\\,{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=14$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399751} :answer-field/id #uuid "9feed68a-3a5e-5bf8-a32b-19c5c668ac59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac39442-17f5-5ace-9954-931f61698611" :question/math-academy-id "q-112457" :question/problem "The equation ${t}^{2}-14t+5=5$ can be written in the form $(t+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${t}_{1}+{t}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-14t+5 & =5 \\\\ {t}^{2}-14t & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2\\cdot t\\cdot 7+{7}^{2}-{7}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-7{)}^{2}-{7}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-7{)}^{2} & =49\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-7$ and $q=49.$ Thus, $pq=-343.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(t-7{)}^{2} & =49 \\\\ \\sqrt{(t-7{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\ |t-7| & =7 \\\\ t-7 & =\\pm 7 \\\\ t & =7\\pm 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =7-7=0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =7+7=14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${t}_{1}+{t}_{2}=14.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c39a0e2e-7b56-5be7-b30b-746a3a4150cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12,\\,m=-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399754} {:answer/id #uuid "015db316-4ebb-5b1d-99f9-48da4423ea95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10,\\,m=-2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399755} {:answer/id #uuid "6bce9687-7b54-57ab-bfdf-8d118e548ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8,\\,m=4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399756} {:answer/id #uuid "b47af5b9-db68-5705-87ff-a810d0b94396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10,\\,m=4\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399757} {:answer/id #uuid "51890425-293e-58e0-898f-8746f49fa609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=14,\\,m=-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c39a0e2e-7b56-5be7-b30b-746a3a4150cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12,\\,m=-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399754} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4fba57-c484-5fd0-a729-550a53e648f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d70e8a4-cad6-5c6f-b225-88fe32f8d423" :question/math-academy-id "q-112487" :question/problem "The equation ${m}^{2}+8=-8m$ can be written in the form $(m+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-8m$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+8 & =-8m \\\\ {m}^{2}+8m & =-8 \\\\ {m}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot m & =-8 \\\\ {m}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot m+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (m+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+4)^{2}-{4}^{2} & =-8 \\\\ (m+4)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=4,q=8,$ and $p+q=12.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+4)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\sqrt{(m+4)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ |m+4| & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ m+4 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ m & =-4\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4405950-ec79-55ae-bb59-7e9cf1458d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399761} {:answer/id #uuid "94d2993f-9616-511b-a59a-68769f0f70be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399762} {:answer/id #uuid "0b4529ef-30af-5714-a478-69c3cf9b6357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399763} {:answer/id #uuid "102384b8-4bb4-5240-aab6-c8e2bfcdf99c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399764} {:answer/id #uuid "419d4d42-f167-5d71-b60f-b6aab67523fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "419d4d42-f167-5d71-b60f-b6aab67523fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399765} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ed4133-1a4b-5aa3-a8c2-be10955c2a4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6742def-6999-5043-9553-f817f1ed7b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-112500" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,J=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A=PJ{P}^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,z=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J={J}_{2}(-2).$  \n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(-2) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-2.$ \n\nNow, computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAmong the given vectors, only $x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2,$ since\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=y,\n$$\n\nand, therefore,\n\n$$\n(A-(-2)I)^{2}x=(A+2I)y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\overset{A-(-2)I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b361b7f6-2975-5ed2-b3d6-cb981bcbc5f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399768} {:answer/id #uuid "af436aca-c25b-50d1-ae25-749ecc05a446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399769} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4e5843-97cc-5c3b-b643-9e3130891ce7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399770} {:answer/id #uuid "04e15661-c522-5a93-a20e-dafb0bf87524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399771} {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab45afe2-3455-59a3-8889-5adbe62eaacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399772} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5f1bfdb-7442-5063-835b-afe41546d21e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa71aa3-e92b-5db0-96a6-7043b42bf47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112524" :question/problem "The equation $4{x}^{2}+6x-5=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-5 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =5 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =5 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{29}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ and $q=\\frac{29}{4}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\np+q=\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{29}{4}=\\frac{32}{4}=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51235c4b-9ea8-5be6-9202-8faae512d7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399775} {:answer/id #uuid "18c50e74-54e2-5b08-8781-bdac4e3eb829" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399776} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef860dc-5a1c-5a5d-8cba-4a4b862a9b98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399777} {:answer/id #uuid "c6502671-64f9-549c-ad24-6af57af49c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399778} {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399779} :answer-field/id #uuid "132377e4-a98b-5f45-bb48-4f5451b52524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0945056-0521-55d9-80fa-5db83ff53407" :question/math-academy-id "q-112612" :question/problem "The equation $-4{z}^{2}-16z-7=0$ can be written in the form $4(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{z}^{2}-16z-7) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z & =-7 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+4z) & =-7 \\\\ 4[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-7 \\\\ 4[{(z+2)}^{2}-4] & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =-7+16 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(z+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |z+2| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "82ccfef3-569a-5ddf-9a69-cae323a0a312" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Possible Roots of a Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dec68e86-8f08-510f-8171-dc1ae0f4f3b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399782} {:answer/id #uuid "1000ee85-77e8-514d-87b6-5fa6ba7c90fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399783} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7fad0-aec1-568c-9216-1773d3768409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399784} {:answer/id #uuid "62c60f36-b157-50e0-b5d9-80c8be74ad99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399785} {:answer/id #uuid "1e872297-3c68-5e8b-85d5-47d2da2edc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e872297-3c68-5e8b-85d5-47d2da2edc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399786} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a9f0fc-0a9d-5636-9f7e-da5babeb1d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8da2d1e9-08f2-56a5-bfb7-528f2e2d7e75" :question/math-academy-id "q-112863" :question/problem "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-14?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-14,$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-14}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2=\\frac{-2}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-2)$ is a factor of $-14$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "653503b2-4631-561e-af5e-847bcbdfe8e2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degree of the Numerator is Greater"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1f564d-015d-531a-84ba-220e72e5068b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399789} {:answer/id #uuid "5cefa02b-c23b-5120-bf50-f5e584021c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399790} {:answer/id #uuid "64a8fd7a-b8ce-5f62-8477-ec3c16462673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399791} {:answer/id #uuid "2a57eba1-1adb-5f47-ab81-1ede087ed451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399792} {:answer/id #uuid "f73c86ea-5390-5440-876a-85ab356720c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cefa02b-c23b-5120-bf50-f5e584021c12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No horizontal asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0eba56c-3f43-5bc0-93ac-9b6c1e733e11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6076d1df-0a51-5be4-8eb1-e4f9aaadd8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-113176" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{6}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{8}-4{x}^{4}+2x}{{x}^{6}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{8}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{4{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ $\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{5}})}{(1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{{x}^{2}-0+0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac5d4b19-8c81-5ba1-996a-70b59878a1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-28,\\,x=3\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399796} {:answer/id #uuid "6512548c-dca0-5e35-9fae-cb9552f71f4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=22,\\,x=3\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399797} {:answer/id #uuid "ea660700-9472-591c-8a60-b82ac1eb0a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-28,\\,x=5\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399798} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2d981b-ddf0-5166-b28f-034d57bae937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=22,\\,x=5\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399799} {:answer/id #uuid "282a88ee-2e7c-579c-b7aa-4a73cb82ac9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-28,\\,x=3\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5d4b19-8c81-5ba1-996a-70b59878a1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-28,\\,x=3\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399796} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb7896d-c7f4-5b7b-a55d-ac98d8904d0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed379096-4def-51e1-8cd6-e568b470e309" :question/math-academy-id "q-113245" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-6x+34=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+34 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x & =-34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x & =-34 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2} & =-34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}-9 & =-34 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-3,\\,q=-25,$ and $p+q=-28.$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{-25} \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 5\\,\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 5\\,\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+5\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-5\\,\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a12e93a-5479-5bc8-a172-5f001b81680d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399803} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd56062-f5eb-5d51-97c9-2401cc2382c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399804} {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399805} {:answer/id #uuid "060d6765-1a4c-5cef-9036-baa44df95b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399806} {:answer/id #uuid "0532888d-2587-5d82-aa30-da64f92b3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ and $x=6$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50434bd2-8dc8-5df0-b8fa-4d7fa1f9bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399805} :answer-field/id #uuid "9de2cff7-92c8-5c70-9102-18ed3f31a5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "046b8096-a4b3-5639-81fd-05cfaf3fcc14" :question/math-academy-id "q-113527" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+6x-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)+6(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+6)(x-2)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-2=0$ gives the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+6=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9bdca2-12f3-5135-add9-f6cee60b6017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399810} {:answer/id #uuid "ea637b56-a1b6-530a-b8ad-0581d7725118" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-\\sqrt{3},x=0$ and $x=\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399811} {:answer/id #uuid "6caf38b1-8d42-5322-9446-2276f113b03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\sqrt{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399812} {:answer/id #uuid "88004c88-6f69-578a-840b-c594bbb79e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399813} {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aeb7239-f064-571a-82f0-341dafcdd4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399814} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce9f7db0-7e00-5308-89b8-2a0dcddb1466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0085fda-c140-5022-a903-4aa2d0060987" :question/math-academy-id "q-113554" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-6}{{x}^{4}-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2({x}^{2}-3)}{({x}^{2}-3)({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}}{\\cancel{({x}^{2}-3)}({x}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd4abd5d-2642-5f0d-af2f-5ed5c265526a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86d5db80-994e-5702-8c1d-e12a82935e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}+10x+55\\,$ and $\\,9{x}^{2}+2x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399817} {:answer/id #uuid "6ca4866b-d86b-545d-bf2a-54b1083ad0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}+11x+45\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}+x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399818} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d8695e-5196-5de3-8a7b-43fe3997211b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}+11x-35\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}-x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399819} {:answer/id #uuid "07b0d23d-ec2d-59ef-9ff0-7ee1b6fd0027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}+10x+45\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}-x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399820} {:answer/id #uuid "27453f3a-03ff-59ee-9447-15e61177956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}-11x+45\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}-2x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ca4866b-d86b-545d-bf2a-54b1083ad0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4{x}^{2}+11x+45\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}+x-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399818} :answer-field/id #uuid "0867db7a-c2e5-5027-95ed-b5d29db04306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb3fd947-7e73-5d82-83d8-1c4c922146ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-113746" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x-4\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x-5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{11{x}^{2}-44x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,45x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{45x-180}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,175\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+11x+45 \\\\  & x-4\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+x-5\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,11{x}^{2}+x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{11{x}^{2}-44x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,45x-5 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{45x-180}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,175\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are \n\n$\\,$ $4{x}^{2}+11x+45\\,$ and $\\,11{x}^{2}+x-5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399822 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Are Neither Local Minima nor Local Maxima"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2b2d3d-bc75-52df-bb2c-abdcbe9305ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399824} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4bfed4-7246-5315-9221-f89dd61376e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399825} {:answer/id #uuid "a324b8bd-f18d-5775-a6a5-02c9e56e4b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399826} {:answer/id #uuid "d961280f-42a3-5c74-8167-a78423307df5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399827} {:answer/id #uuid "2b36fc7f-e874-5627-a39c-c2930cf8cca1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a324b8bd-f18d-5775-a6a5-02c9e56e4b3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399826} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f06b88a-0b3e-517c-8884-684e65abf6f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5d605cec-3072-5304-adcf-70f0f4b40d1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113767" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{e}^{x}},$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x{e}^{x}-({x}^{2}+1){e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{e}^{x}}\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{{e}^{0}}=-1<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{(2-1)^{2}}{{e}^{2}}=-{e}^{-2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399829 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26c342f1-881b-5f3e-8c76-448eec165168" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Polynomial With Some Zero Coefficients by a Linear Binomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6409232-2d31-593b-90bb-8a5813883aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x+48\\,$ and $\\,48x-8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399831} {:answer/id #uuid "8add39dc-99a4-5ff4-9055-09c5f3363d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x-24\\,$ and $\\,24x-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399832} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8cd1cd-1378-56d5-a7ab-7daa3b9b05fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x+30\\,$ and $\\,30x-6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399833} {:answer/id #uuid "29edcf41-1ad2-52f3-ab8d-9b28e983225b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x-36\\,$ and $\\,48x-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399834} {:answer/id #uuid "f2683559-249d-5d23-9f72-d0c8764dba6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x+36\\,$ and $\\,36x-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2683559-249d-5d23-9f72-d0c8764dba6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6x+36\\,$ and $\\,36x-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399835} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb0dafe-c8dd-5892-9eb8-f305e87ca008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3536c096-9c19-5ecb-8cd6-bf3a33c8475e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114262" :question/problem "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x-6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+0x-4} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-36x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{36x-216}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,212\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows: \n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+0x-4\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+36 \\\\  & x-6\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+0x-4} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-36x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,36x-4 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{36x-216}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,212\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $6x+36\\,$ and $\\,36x-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60d399bd-315a-5c25-b63b-0a7391724a1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399838} {:answer/id #uuid "920e6436-61f2-5a55-8fd8-6326ab8c0807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399839} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e096b7-2688-5afd-8241-00c938c02da0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399840} {:answer/id #uuid "596b5a75-fa8d-518b-b2eb-9eb7b3a09dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399841} {:answer/id #uuid "c210c0cc-3f26-5461-99d3-ddbe6f537e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c210c0cc-3f26-5461-99d3-ddbe6f537e73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399842} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b865a5-78da-5138-9a6e-a4f61421c384" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "959a29f9-94e3-5ec3-bf75-8100c4fac3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114399" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f20b09e85ce71a1001e7c701ebfafa3e6ac6c8adb29e8dfd47f256f37a29f5f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,1.5)$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fdeb0d-d904-56f6-8b96-e74fb1d0181b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399845} {:answer/id #uuid "837da0ae-38ee-520a-bb45-9843ff8b7fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399846} {:answer/id #uuid "5c19d501-1c59-527a-b178-612c02ea093c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399847} {:answer/id #uuid "798ac375-522d-536d-8032-9e69ca6b5bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399848} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbdfd0a-a047-563b-8afb-05847e707b41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "798ac375-522d-536d-8032-9e69ca6b5bf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399848} :answer-field/id #uuid "989f84dd-5950-58e9-b6c6-2ad30ae07799" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "444031a3-3309-59e7-a714-c81365022d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-114402" :question/problem "![](images/20/20b6532fc5d99c74a26bd752e19aa2a6451ea26a79d2a129e4c0203bc9133354.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,4).$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c54fdcb-dd7b-59be-a4a1-0f740a2e2b94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399851} {:answer/id #uuid "76c31317-40e5-598b-9f1c-48b7b99ba737" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399852} {:answer/id #uuid "98942d10-5093-5b9e-b56e-6cb0c197d275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=\\pm 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399853} {:answer/id #uuid "94d7a6b5-dc7f-5665-8396-f95fbd37d4d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399854} {:answer/id #uuid "1d987e38-5562-5ec2-8fa2-95ef362d9640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d987e38-5562-5ec2-8fa2-95ef362d9640" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$g(x)$ has no inflection points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399855} :answer-field/id #uuid "13bd9faa-5e19-585c-9f6b-cfbc5b854865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399850}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47a20fe2-66d7-5e4d-88bc-ebcb0a87e290" :question/math-academy-id "q-114418" :question/problem "![](images/27/27f92e7288aa52ef67a6346fc5101173f51993be77cdd8face0517f2e6e790d3.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ does not cross the $x$-axis. \n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ no inflection points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e43e393-8752-58bc-b87c-b72f679cbfd9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4ffa0ff-df0b-5bc5-b896-3f6772a35019" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ and $24$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399858} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f1177f-3e11-593f-ad9c-dda3299fba50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$24.5$ and $18$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399859} {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399860} {:answer/id #uuid "ee829b75-97f1-545e-8cf4-3704340f4ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$49$ and $36$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399861} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e390e1-9f85-5321-a954-3f147b97e82b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e59ee665-26a4-5d2e-8c55-26143f2480d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14$ and $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399860} :answer-field/id #uuid "44102d96-20a4-51fc-add2-7a7a026df6ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3113828e-6ca6-530b-804d-2cdc91aade73" :question/math-academy-id "q-115134" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=49$ and ${b}^{2}=36.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=49,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=36,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=6.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 6=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec162965-0de8-5d06-b41a-062a9d1f0fad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈2.4,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399865} {:answer/id #uuid "9efeacdb-d497-5e76-9de5-4223deab8a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈2.4,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399866} {:answer/id #uuid "f87e4658-cf1d-5ad2-9e02-63234b51f5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈1.8,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399867} {:answer/id #uuid "4bc78bc2-cf75-59ca-97de-724dc9cb25b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈2.6,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399868} {:answer/id #uuid "65531020-f4a8-5d25-8b9b-42efc66428f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈2.6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9efeacdb-d497-5e76-9de5-4223deab8a82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(0.8)≈2.4,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399866} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a10c896-987b-59f6-9202-743db84c67f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5a82529-6969-5fb0-8f80-d44b22e50fb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115265" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(1,2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(0,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ estimate the value of $f(0.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(1,2),$ so we have $f(1)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(-2)(x-1) \\\\  & =2-2(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.8) & ≈L(0.8) \\\\  & =2-2(0.8-1) \\\\  & =2-2(-0.2) \\\\  & =2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(0,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph close to $x=1,$ and therefore we have an underestimate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399870 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3dd05f-a366-554a-ba55-c8784fb9b96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399872} {:answer/id #uuid "00a4d99b-7934-5f5d-a6b3-5de378407aa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.5$ and $2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399873} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9c641e-6ce8-5759-aed4-632f63149f36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399874} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6352b-c991-5fb7-bffd-80792e4e906d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399875} {:answer/id #uuid "daff1418-4257-51e4-a9d5-dccaf05f42a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daff1418-4257-51e4-a9d5-dccaf05f42a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399876} :answer-field/id #uuid "1872eebc-f1ce-56c5-86dd-93ce7b60f7ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "023cf167-60aa-5f09-9ef1-d06a20d0ee94" :question/math-academy-id "q-115311" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-6$ and $k=-4,$ so the center is located at $(-6,-4).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=5$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=5,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53855cff-bcca-509b-8df4-37d79e809e7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399879} {:answer/id #uuid "17302a0c-db71-5e47-9f9c-7d55635451b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399880} {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399881} {:answer/id #uuid "f17c689d-e9fa-5fd9-b2ff-5ea750ce8ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399882} {:answer/id #uuid "d786fe68-0f02-5397-bafb-ab8b7df3332f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12.5$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6aed6ae-4085-506e-b702-8b9d95570e9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $2\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399881} :answer-field/id #uuid "632f4b79-0b5d-599f-9a9d-155f44ffca58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6821b43a-88ac-5b4b-9151-2f35f973a4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-115313" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{6}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-5$ and $k=2,$ so the center is located at $(-5,2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=25$ and ${b}^{2}=6.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=6,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=2\\sqrt{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f542453-9758-55e6-93c6-6bfd2139ec45" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent Line Approximation and Classifying it as an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0f6466d-f440-58c1-8723-cde1e85101c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399886} {:answer/id #uuid "1906a013-62f9-568e-95ae-98c68b274de9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399887} {:answer/id #uuid "261b2021-339d-51c1-a9b8-1c77073b9824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399888} {:answer/id #uuid "d631bbed-d22a-5f68-8054-c6e4993d2296" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}},$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399889} {:answer/id #uuid "87b365d2-efb1-55b5-ba28-a027ed309d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈5,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "261b2021-339d-51c1-a9b8-1c77073b9824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(\\sqrt{2})≈6,$ an overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399888} :answer-field/id #uuid "4547a518-52cd-55fa-a5a7-64752ebe189c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7b7e632-32b9-5dd9-98c7-3b49b8d1e749" :question/math-academy-id "q-115348" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,5)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ estimate the value of $f(\\sqrt{2})$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(0,5),$ so we have $f(0)=5.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =5+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x-0) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(\\sqrt{2})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\sqrt{2}) & ≈L(\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =5+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =5+1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-\\pi ,\\pi ),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an overestimate."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52384502-66c5-5089-b38f-d46b94877430" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Radius of a Circle on the Coordinate Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8fa6bba-8366-50e3-bd6e-0487665ca2c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399893} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba675a-9578-5d7e-86a6-668cee3df4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399894} {:answer/id #uuid "73d0aee8-773e-58fb-89a9-36098d20a922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,4)$ and the radius is $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399895} {:answer/id #uuid "d14d98e0-0f4f-5aa7-b716-c80926e97a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399896} {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4de2cf95-13f1-5b1b-8916-4cd2aebe378f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The center is $(0,3)$ and the radius is $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399897} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a898ac5-9ac8-57ea-9a3a-e0a38e6b53ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a02e3599-0d24-591c-985f-c45e9f652ee9" :question/math-academy-id "q-115364" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3af3101fd7a103e8382755177ca1ebab9bc1cdf48aebd34af8ac7cc92991387c.png)\n\nGiven that the points $(-1,2)$ and $(1,4)$ lie on a diameter of a circle, what are the center and radius of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The center of the circle is located at the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can calculate the coordinates of the center using the midpoint formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(h,k) & =(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{2+4}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{0}{2},\\frac{6}{2}) \\\\  & =(0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe radius can be found by calculating the distance between the center $(0,3)$ and a point on the circle, such as $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{({x}_{1}-{x}_{2})}^{2}+{({y}_{1}-{y}_{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-0)}^{2}+{(2-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167091 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fba5f68e-15da-5804-b6d9-d72ce6acae59" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field by Identifying Asymptotic Behavior"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8be033b5-a5be-5124-becb-8216baa101f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399899} {:answer/id #uuid "be719de0-5aff-5f76-b0c3-483d2a500723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399900} {:answer/id #uuid "ff967d2c-319e-592a-bbcf-b26e98cc48b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399901} {:answer/id #uuid "e83ac51a-0e3f-5990-8951-a42760df6d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399902} {:answer/id #uuid "abd37abd-f6f8-5480-bcc0-29ccc8c8f535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e83ac51a-0e3f-5990-8951-a42760df6d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399902} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99c72e-4b83-5737-a710-a4529e2905bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "85b66c06-8b49-5d46-861f-2424f521305f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115479" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b608fabe99c6062f9d9f04cc7f3b2933e453812ed4ce9783908666c71c1c74b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$\nII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7bb52825944a7515a6281a88b5bc740cf6d097380fa13fe7e00efc5e499d49f8.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $y\\to 0.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field are positive everywhere.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{y-1}=\\frac{1}{(1-\\frac{1}{y})}\\to 1\\,\\text{as}\\,y\\to \\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slopes of the slope field for this differential equation do not become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are negative for $y<0.$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}$ gives the required behavior. Notice that ${y}^{′}\\to 0$ as $y\\to \\infty ,$ and ${y}^{′}\\to \\infty $ as $y\\to 0.$ Also, the right-hand side of this equation is positive for all $y\\ne 0.$"}]}
